      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
